 :finding-responses
 [{:id :scope-policy-not-yet-defined
   :decision "Use an explicit private parser-options stack of function frames and an explicit traversal phase; no mutable or process-global name table. The scope restriction below intentionally changes behavior only for nested bodies under a newly named outer function when a legacy free symbol has exactly the same spelling as that outer binder: reject as ambiguous capture, never silently reinterpret. Independent symbols of other spellings remain legacy. This conservative collision rejection is part of the admitted new syntax, not a claim to preserve every ambiguous combination."
   :algorithm ["Each parse-function allocates a fresh frame F={bindings name->index, named? boolean}; ancestors are inherited as frames, never merged. Sibling IFn alternatives receive identical parent opts but never one another's frame. Parent bindings are not in the type-variable/free environment."
               "Parse this function's domain in phase :domain with a barrier at its new frame: current binders are unavailable; inherited names are capture candidates, not resolvable indices. Parse range, filters including types inside is/!, and map object in phase :body with F current. Recursive parse-type preserves this frame until another parse-function pushes its own barrier and frame."
               "At each object-id or proposition-subject boundary in :body: a symbol found in current F lowers to F's index, even when an ancestor uses the same spelling (inner shadows outer). Otherwise if it occurs in any ancestor frame, signal unsupported-outer-capture; otherwise if F is named? signal unknown-subject; otherwise retain the legacy symbol unchanged. Literal integers and non-symbol values follow the original path unchanged. A named current frame rejects any other free symbol; an unnamed current frame retains unrelated free symbols."
               "During nested function domain traversal, apply the same capture guard to ancestor binder spellings at object/proposition subject boundaries but allow unrelated legacy symbols; never bind a nested domain occurrence to its not-yet-in-scope parameter. Outside all new named frames preserve old behavior. Root-level standalone object parsing and unrelated functions do not become strict."
               "Thus inner named same-name wins; inner unnamed use of outer name fails; independent inner unnamed legacy subject z survives; sibling name in a named arity is unknown, not inherited. Object ids and proposition subjects in nested function types inside a range or filter type use the same barriers. Existing opts callers without markers retain historical behavior."]}
  {:id :fixed-prefix-classification-must-follow-parse-cat
   :decision "Support a conservative syntactic and parsed mandatory fixed prefix; reject rather than estimate width. Do not claim parse-ast grammar parity beyond its actual fixed raw-dom."
   :algorithm ["If no top-level :- token appears in the function domain, invoke the original parse-cat exactly once with the original token vector and opts; do not alter grammar, result or warnings. In a named domain, scan the same top-level tokens and postfix associations as parse-cat, treating nested collections as opaque type syntax; recognize only complete simple-symbol :- type triples. Diagnose malformed binder markers before type resolution."
               "Associate immediately following :? :+ :* * <* :.. <... :... ... with the preceding type component according to parse-cat's actual context-sensitive rules, and & with its keyword tail. A named component with any such postfix is rejected as named-variable-component, even where a deprecated operator would otherwise be treated as a type; no binder is stripped first and no rest name is invented."
               "Parse each candidate prefix type exactly once with the same allow-regex and original opts used by parse-cat; it proves width one only if the parsed component is r/Type? and not r/Regex?, r/DottedPretype? or r/KwArgs?, and has no associated postfix. Conservatively treat every regex, including fixed-width-looking cat/alt, as unproved. Increment position only for proven prefix components; a binder at or after the first unproved component fails binder-after-variable-component. This also rejects a named component whose type itself parses to a regex."
               "After the last named component, preserve the remaining original token stream and parse-cat operator handling, including :.. bounds and deprecated * warnings, without reparsing the prefix or duplicating side effects. Refactor parse-cat locally to consume classified components or a shared one-pass parser, not by calling parse-type and then parse-cat again. Compare the resulting cat-dom and warning stream to an unnamed equivalent. If one-pass reuse cannot preserve exact behavior under the file ceiling, stop for review."
               "Runtime raw-dom admits ordinary fixed triples before final unnamed *, :.., <*, <... or &; the checker may have wider regex syntax but this slice does not imply runtime parity for :?/:+/:* or nested regex. The exact positive dotted spelling is (t/All [x :..] [a :- t/Any x :.. x :-> t/Any :object {:id a}])."]}
  {:id :positional-lowering-and-test-contract-incomplete
   :decision "Lower at parser boundaries, once, within the current lexical frame. The source literal is checked for warning before any lowering; no tree walk, string rewrite, global warning suppression, Lexical object, or abo/abstract-result conversion."
   :algorithm ["parse-object accepts existing empty/no-object and map syntax only. For map id, preserve original literal validation and integer deprecation path. For a symbolic id, resolve with current frame and pass the returned internal index directly to orep/-path; never feed the generated integer back through literal parse-object-path. Preserve path element parse and order. Runtime shorthand :object a is not newly supported."
               "Resolve the subject of is and ! through that same frame resolver immediately before the existing proposition constructors, after parsing their type and optional path; literal integer proposition subjects retain their old no-warning behavior. Recursive and/or/when use existing parser recursion, not a post-hoc result walk. All preexisting numeric/symbol IDs in nested functions remain as written unless they are the named subject at the current boundary."
               "A named index is local to its function Result and is not shifted at parse time. For a nested function, its own id 0 must remain 0: subst-obj's Function fold shifts the outer substitution key by inner arg-count when traversing its range; thus an outer call should not substitute the inner id. Outer named captures are rejected, so no new cross-frame index arithmetic is promised. Preserve existing explicitly written integer IDs, including manually encoded outer offsets; no shifting or remapping them. Nested range and filter-type applications must compare named and literal positional equivalents and check both outer-then-inner call behavior. If evidence shows existing substitution fails this parity or needs repair outside parser, stop and obtain reviewed file-scope expansion rather than assert soundness."
               "Check path composition via real open-Result and real application: object caller path is prepended to latent path by subst-object; proposition has its own source order and must be compared to legacy behavior rather than assumed identical. Application proofs distinguish actual arg 1 from arg 0; inspect Result fields as well as typechecking."]}
  {:id :red-oracle-must-distinguish-rejection-reasons
   :decision "Positive feature assertions are RED on baseline and GREEN on candidate; guard cases assert stable unique diagnostics and may already throw for a different baseline reason. Run identical tests on both heads only after separately authorized execution. Record exact baseline outcomes; candidate negatives must prove their specific reason and must fail against an unsafe candidate that removes each guard."
   :diagnostics {:duplicate "named-binder/duplicate" :malformed "named-binder/malformed" :unknown "named-binder/unknown-subject" :capture "named-binder/unsupported-outer-capture" :named-variable "named-binder/named-variable-component" :after-variable "named-binder/binder-after-variable-component"}
   :assertion "Use prs-error with these stable tokens in messages (or structured ex-data with exactly these reason codes if existing error infrastructure demonstrably preserves it); tests assert token and subject, and explicitly reject Cannot resolve type: <binder> as the intended failure. Capture exact error through the established test infrastructure; do not guess exception class. Sibling unknown uses unknown-subject because sibling bindings are never visible."}]
